name exposure_sweep
describe Goal 237's check: look up at the bright sky, down into a shadowed valley, and back, capturing at both extremes. The crosshair-metered adaptation should follow the crosshair rather than the frame, and it should do so ASYMMETRICALLY -- fast when the view brightens, slow when it darkens, which is the light/dark adaptation asymmetry a two-timescale filter gets for free. The exposure trace at every capture is in the log, which is where this scenario's real output lives; the images are for eyeballing.
backend both

option --fly
option --noclip
option --no-taa

pose_ground 48,0 -90 -6 24

# Up into open sky: nothing but the gradient, so the metered region is the brightest the world has.
look -90 60 1
wait 3
capture time 3.5 sky_adapted no-golden

# Then down at the ground. DARKENING, so the slow constant applies and a capture two seconds in
# should still be visibly mid-adaptation -- that lag IS the measurement.
look -90 -75 1
capture time 5.6 valley_entering no-golden
wait 4
capture time 9.5 valley_adapted no-golden

# And back up. BRIGHTENING, so the fast constant applies and the same two seconds is nearly settled.
look -90 60 1
capture time 11.2 sky_returning no-golden
wait 4

capture end sky_readapted no-golden

assert frames > 0
assert contrast_percent > 6
